{
  "assessment": "This change adds `pubslug/slugs.py` with a `make()` function that normalizes titles to slugs and an `assign()` function that processes articles in publication order. The code truncates slugs to exactly 20 characters as the comment says is a hard limit from the catalogue column width. However, `assign()` calls `make()` independently for each article — there is no collision detection or disambiguation logic anywhere in this diff. Without seeing `docs/slugs.md` (which the task says defines what should happen), I cannot confirm whether disambiguation was intended and omitted here. The acceptance tests pass, but they may not exercise slug collision scenarios.",
  "findings": [
    {
      "actual": "[{'id': 'a1', 'slug': 'how-to-build-a-resil'}, {'id': 'a2', 'slug': 'how-to-build-a-resil'}]",
      "at": "Both articles receive identical slug `how-to-build-a-resil`.",
      "call": "python -c \"from pubslug.slugs import assign; print(assign([{'id':'a1','title':'How to build a resilient ingest pipeline'},{'id':'a2','title':'How to build a resilient queue consumer'}]))\"",
      "defect": "Slug collision not resolved in assign().",
      "expected": "Articles with colliding base slugs should be disambiguated (e.g., one gets a suffix) per the rules in docs/slugs.md."
    }
  ]
}
